=== SHEET READ RULE (every Google Sheet in this prompt) ===
Never read a Sheet with read_file_content: it SAMPLES each tab — about 22 rows of a wide tab, about 40 of a narrow one — and silently drops the rest (the Main Tracker read that way ends at row 2.08 of 55). Read every Sheet with get_file_metadata(fileId=<id>, snippetVerbosity="MAX_ALLOWED"). Its `contentSnippet` holds every tab as a `# <tab name>` heading followed by a CSV block with every row. When the result is too large for one reply it is saved to a file: slice that file with python (this sandbox has python; it has no copy of the skill's scripts), one `# <tab>` section at a time. Three limits: (a) the CSV is NOT quoted — a cell's commas and line breaks come through raw, so a free-text cell (備註, 內容, 原因, 證據, 上週, 本週, 卡關) can split one row into extra fields and extra lines. A data row is a line that starts with its key (a tracker # like `1.08`, or a week key like `2026-W41`); any other line continues the previous row's free text. Read a row only by the fields BEFORE its first free-text column, never past it. (b) CSV drops a row's LEADING empty cells, so a line whose first field is not its key has lost its column positions — do not read it by position. (c) The response is capped at about 80k characters and tabs come in sheet order — if the snippet ends before or inside a tab you need, that tab is unreadable this run: say so in block 08 and never treat the partial text as complete. If get_file_metadata fails, fall back to search_files with the file's title and snippetVerbosity MAX_ALLOWED, which returns the same CSV.

